      "problem": "Find the value of $k$ if the remainder of the division $\\frac{{x}^{3}+kx+1}{x+1}$ is $0.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $q(x)={x}^{3}+kx+1.$ \n\nThe remainder theorem states that when $q(x)$ is divided by $(x+1),$ the remainder equals $q(-1).$ Since we're told that the remainder is $0,$ then, we must have $q(-1)=0.$\n\nEvaluating $q(-1)$ directly gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(-1) & =(-1)^{3}+k(-1)+1 \\\\  & =-1-k+1 \\\\  & =-k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $k=0.$",
